 {"id": "gold", "slug": "ideas/gold-nanoparticles-cancer-treatment", "t": "Fighting cancer with ... gold?!?!", "d": "Researchers in Nova Scotia think superheated nanorods could help take on melanoma", "img": "https://cdn.sanity.io/images/l3tzmu37/production/d4aefdf613204969e78603479b40d9a68da6bc9f-1350x1350.png?auto=format&fit=max&w=1000&q=70", "p": "Ideas", "s": "", "tags": ["Science & tech", "Health"], "a": "Sarah Hampson", "date": "April 2, 2026", "m": 7, "body": ["In a small, cluttered office at St. Francis Xavier University in Nova Scotia, two scientists are excitedly explaining why they think they can fight cancer with gold. Specifically, they\u2019ve created a particular gold nanoparticle \u2013 a rod of tiny atoms of gold \u2013 that can be aimed at cancer cells and then superheated to destroy them. Early results have shown promise and turbocharged the researchers\u2019 enthusiasm."]},
 {"id": "milica", "slug": "people/milica-radisic-microscopic-human-organs", "t": "The mind-boggling Toronto lab growing tiny human hearts", "d": "Milica Radisic\u2019s team is creating little kidneys, mini brains and beating hearts that could speed up life-saving drug testing", "img": "https://cdn.sanity.io/images/l3tzmu37/production/73d4befc745d21b13a325d242a9a75601bab3622-1440x840.webp?auto=format&fit=max&w=1000&q=70", "p": "People", "s": "", "tags": ["Health", "Science & tech"], "a": "Luc Rinaldi", "date": "April 2, 2026", "m": 11, "body": ["Moore\u2019s Law, that famous maxim of computer science, holds that chips double in power every two years. The field of medicine is subject to a more demoralizing axiom: Eroom\u2019s Law (that\u2019s Moore backwards), which dictates that the life-saving science of drug discovery gets slower and more expensive with time, doubling in cost every nine years. It now takes an average of 12 years and $1 billion \u2013 and counting \u2013 to bring a new medicine to market."]}
